WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.048
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:08.874
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:15.077
Unit twelve: Diplomatic History. Today you will recount history using the past passive.

4
00:00:15.377 --> 00:00:18.157
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.333
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:24.266
Diplomacy.

7
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:29.235
Diplomacy means the management of relations between states by negotiation.

8
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.861
Quiet diplomacy ended the standoff.

9
00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.091
Your turn. Say: Diplomacy.

10
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:38.969
Diplomacy.

11
00:00:39.717 --> 00:00:40.428
Treaty.

12
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:44.531
Treaty means a formal written agreement between states.

13
00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:47.790
The treaty was ratified by both parliaments.

14
00:00:48.383 --> 00:00:50.618
Your turn. Say: Treaty.

15
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:54.212
Treaty.

16
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:56.067
Alliance.

17
00:00:57.083 --> 00:01:01.451
Alliance means a formal partnership between states for mutual defence.

18
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.885
The alliance committed members to defend one another.

19
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:08.275
Your turn. Say: Alliance.

20
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:12.367
Alliance.

21
00:01:13.117 --> 00:01:14.524
Balance of power.

22
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:19.931
Balance of power means a distribution of strength that discourages dominance.

23
00:01:20.517 --> 00:01:23.573
Europe pursued a balance of power for centuries.

24
00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:27.103
Your turn. Say: Balance of power.

25
00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:31.432
Balance of power.

26
00:01:32.183 --> 00:01:33.393
Negotiation.

27
00:01:34.383 --> 00:01:37.474
Negotiation means discussion aimed at reaching agreement.

28
00:01:38.083 --> 00:01:40.570
Months of negotiation produced the accord.

29
00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:43.673
Your turn. Say: Negotiation.

30
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:47.767
Negotiation.

31
00:01:48.517 --> 00:01:50.121
Sphere of influence.

32
00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:56.506
Sphere of influence means a region in which one state holds special power.

33
00:01:57.117 --> 00:02:00.347
The empire claimed the region as its sphere of influence.

34
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.055
Your turn. Say: Sphere of influence.

35
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.656
Sphere of influence.

36
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:10.894
Diplomatic immunity.

37
00:02:11.883 --> 00:02:16.425
Diplomatic immunity means protection of diplomats from prosecution in the host country.

38
00:02:17.017 --> 00:02:20.537
Diplomatic immunity allows envoys to work without fear.

39
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.104
Your turn. Say: Diplomatic immunity.

40
00:02:26.967 --> 00:02:28.699
Diplomatic immunity.

41
00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:30.602
Statecraft.

42
00:02:31.617 --> 00:02:35.091
Statecraft means the skilful management of state affairs.

43
00:02:35.683 --> 00:02:39.645
Metternich's statecraft shaped nineteenth-century Europe.

44
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.111
Your turn. Say: Statecraft.

45
00:02:45.967 --> 00:02:46.852
Statecraft.

46
00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:51.155
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:52.017 --> 00:02:57.430
Visal, why did so many European countries sign alliances before 1914?

48
00:02:58.517 --> 00:03:06.855
Many alliances were formed to maintain the balance of power, and secret promises were exchanged among the great powers.

49
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.148
Did the system work?

50
00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:20.890
For decades it did. But when the alliances were triggered by a single assassination, a local crisis was transformed into a continental war.

51
00:03:21.983 --> 00:03:23.217
What was learned?

52
00:03:24.317 --> 00:03:35.883
After 1945 institutions were established so that disputes could be settled by negotiation, and treaties were designed to be enforceable.

53
00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:38.708
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:43.314
Grammar focus: Past passive for historical events.

55
00:03:43.614 --> 00:03:44.790
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.483 --> 00:03:48.923
Many alliances were formed to maintain the balance of power.

57
00:03:49.817 --> 00:03:53.244
A local crisis was transformed into a continental war.

58
00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:58.715
Institutions were established after 1945.

59
00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:09.180
Past passive = was/were + past participle. It foregrounds events and hides actors, which suits historical writing: Treaties were signed, borders were redrawn.

60
00:04:09.917 --> 00:04:14.252
Be careful! Don't say: Many alliances formed to keep peace.

61
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.604
Say: Many alliances were formed to keep peace.

62
00:04:19.904 --> 00:04:22.183
Past passive: was/were + p.p.

63
00:04:22.967 --> 00:04:25.491
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:27.557
The Making of an Order.

65
00:04:27.857 --> 00:04:33.177
Diplomatic history studies how states have managed their relations through treaties, alliances and war.

66
00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:41.298
After the Napoleonic Wars, the Congress of Vienna produced a balance of power that kept the peace among the great powers for decades.

67
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:48.584
Many alliances were formed in the following century, and spheres of influence were marked out across Africa and Asia.

68
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:57.753
In 1914 a local crisis was transformed into a world war because rigid commitments were triggered one after another.

69
00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:07.036
After 1945 new institutions were established, and statecraft was redirected toward negotiation and collective security.

70
00:05:08.133 --> 00:05:12.263
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:13.117 --> 00:05:16.591
Many alliances blank formed to maintain the balance of power.

72
00:05:16.891 --> 00:05:19.743
A, were. B, was. C, have.

73
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:24.684
The answer is A: were.

74
00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:29.022
A formal written agreement between states is a blank.

75
00:05:29.322 --> 00:05:32.987
A, treaty. B, sphere. C, statecraft.

76
00:05:36.533 --> 00:05:38.266
The answer is A: treaty.

77
00:05:39.417 --> 00:05:42.508
Protection of diplomats from prosecution is blank.

78
00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:47.506
A, negotiation. B, diplomatic immunity. C, alliance.

79
00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:53.426
The answer is B: diplomatic immunity.

80
00:05:54.583 --> 00:05:56.258
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:56.558 --> 00:06:08.234
A, The treaty signed in 1648.. B, The treaty was signed in 1648.. C, The treaty was sign in 1648..

82
00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:16.679
The answer is B: The treaty was signed in 1648..

83
00:06:17.817 --> 00:06:22.475
According to the reading, after 1945 statecraft was redirected toward…

84
00:06:22.775 --> 00:06:28.112
A, empire. B, negotiation and collective security. C, secret pacts.

85
00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:35.501
The answer is B: negotiation and collective security.

86
00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.840
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:40.683 --> 00:06:44.088
What historical treaty do you think had the greatest lasting impact?

88
00:07:00.883 --> 00:07:02.430
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:31.115
I think the Treaty of Westphalia in 1648 had the greatest lasting impact. It established the principle that each state is sovereign within its territory, an idea that still organises international politics. The conflicts it ended were bloody, and the system it created was imperfect. Yet modern borders and diplomacy are built on its foundations.

90
00:07:31.415 --> 00:07:33.113
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:34.017 --> 00:07:38.863
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and past passive for historical events.

92
00:07:39.163 --> 00:07:44.149
Today's value is historical awareness. Know the past so you can understand the present.

93
00:07:44.449 --> 00:07:47.113
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:47.413 --> 00:07:50.565
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

